course: dictionary
lesson: 15069
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word rick is one of them. When you meet it, read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «The farmer built a tall rick of hay in the field.» Here the words hay and field tell you that a rick is a big stack of hay.
explain: Rick can also be a verb, a doing word. «Sam slept badly on the sofa and ricked his neck.» Sam could not turn his head, so to rick your neck means to twist or strain it a little, so that it hurts. This is an informal word, the kind people use in everyday talk in Britain.
explain: Rick can also be a name, with a capital letter. «Rick is my best friend.» Rick is a short form of Richard, Eric or Frederick. A capital letter at the start is a clue that it is a name, not a stack of hay.
fact: A rick is a big stack of hay, straw or corn built in a field or farmyard.
fact: Ricks often had a pointed, thatched top to keep the rain out.
fact: In old country stories, «the mice ran into the rick to hide» because ricks were places where small animals lived.
fact: To rick your neck or back (informal, UK) means to twist or strain it a little, so it hurts.
fact: Rick is also a short name, from Richard, Eric or Frederick. A capital letter is a clue that it is a name.
exercise: «The farmer built a tall rick of hay in the field.» What is a rick here? | A gate into the farmyard | A kind of tractor | A small brown mouse | * A big stack of hay
exercise: «Sam slept badly on the sofa and ricked his neck.» What does ricked mean here? | Fell asleep happily | Ran very fast | * Twisted or strained, so it hurts | Built a stack of hay
exercise: «Rick is my best friend.» What kind of word is Rick here? | A stack of hay | A thatched roof | * A name, short for Richard, Eric or Frederick | A kind of farm animal
test: «The mice ran into the rick to hide from the cat.» Where did the mice hide? | In the river | * In the rick | Up a tree | Under the bed
test: «Dad said, "Don't lift that box or you'll rick your back."» What might happen if you rick your back? | You catch a cold | * You twist or strain it, so it hurts | You grow taller | You fall into a stack of hay
exam: «After the harvest, the farmer's ricks stood tall in the yard, each with a pointed thatched top.» What was the top of each rick made of? | * Thatch | Glass | Metal bars | Paper
